1
00:00:00,650 --> 00:00:05,816
We’ll combine a Gaussian-reconstructed
environment with a volumetric performer in Unity,

2
00:00:05,816 --> 00:00:09,103
then add navigation, proximity activation and
teleportation.

3
00:00:12,650 --> 00:00:15,198
Gsplat renders the static environment.

4
00:00:15,198 --> 00:00:17,746
DynGsplat plays the dynamic sequence.

5
00:00:17,746 --> 00:00:21,823
DualGS provides the human reconstruction and data
workflow.

6
00:00:24,650 --> 00:00:30,077
The first option is to use pretrained Gaussian
PLY models linked in the official 3DGS

7
00:00:30,077 --> 00:00:30,439
repository.

8
00:00:32,650 --> 00:00:35,907
The second option is to get a scene from
SuperSplat.

9
00:00:35,907 --> 00:00:38,513
The DREAMS lab used here is available there.

10
00:00:42,650 --> 00:00:48,425
For a SOG scene, open it in the SuperSplat
editor, choose PLY in the export panel, and

11
00:00:48,425 --> 00:00:49,783
turn off Compress PLY.

12
00:00:54,650 --> 00:01:00,001
You can also create your own scene with a phone,
using apps such as Scaniverse, Polycam

13
00:01:00,001 --> 00:01:01,005
or KIRI Engine.

14
00:01:01,005 --> 00:01:05,687
Export the reconstructed Gaussian scene in a
compatible format and import it into Unity.

15
00:01:09,650 --> 00:01:14,860
For clips such as Bass, follow the dataset links
in the DualGS repository.

16
00:01:14,860 --> 00:01:19,268
Reconstruct the multi-view recordings with
DualGS, then compress the appearance Gaussian

17
00:01:19,268 --> 00:01:24,077
PLY sequence with DynGsplat into DGS metadata and
data blocks for Unity.

18
00:01:24,077 --> 00:01:30,088
A prepared DGS sequence from the authors or
course team, when available, can be imported

19
00:01:30,088 --> 00:01:30,489
directly.

20
00:01:30,489 --> 00:01:33,695
This demo uses an existing preprocessed Bass
sequence.

21
00:01:35,317 --> 00:01:38,118
Create a 3D project in Unity Hub.

22
00:01:38,118 --> 00:01:40,920
This example uses the Built-in Render Pipeline.

23
00:01:51,317 --> 00:01:57,376
For this desktop setup, select Direct3D 12 as the
graphics API and Gamma as the color

24
00:01:57,376 --> 00:01:57,755
space.

25
00:02:03,317 --> 00:02:07,895
Open Package Manager and install Gsplat first,
followed by DynGsplat.

26
00:02:07,895 --> 00:02:13,847
This example uses existing local packages: choose
Install package from disk and select

27
00:02:13,847 --> 00:02:15,679
each package’s package.json file.

28
00:02:23,317 --> 00:02:28,280
Before importing the dynamic sequence, open
Window, Asset Management, Addressables,

29
00:02:28,280 --> 00:02:28,777
Groups.

30
00:02:28,777 --> 00:02:33,244
Create Addressables settings if they have not
been initialized.

31
00:02:38,317 --> 00:02:42,538
Place the Gaussian PLY inside Assets and let
Unity import it.

32
00:02:42,538 --> 00:02:47,526
Create an environment object, add a Gsplat
Renderer, and assign the imported Gsplat

33
00:02:47,526 --> 00:02:47,910
Asset.

34
00:02:56,317 --> 00:03:01,361
Import the complete performer folder, keeping the
DGS metadata and all data blocks

35
00:03:01,361 --> 00:03:02,525
together, outside Resources.

36
00:03:02,525 --> 00:03:07,569
Add a DynGsplat Renderer to the performer object
and assign the dynamic asset.

37
00:03:15,317 --> 00:03:18,188
Check the up axis, scale and ground alignment.

38
00:03:18,188 --> 00:03:22,136
Use a sign or another asymmetric landmark to
check for mirroring.

39
00:03:22,136 --> 00:03:26,084
Place the performer’s feet on the floor and
adjust its orientation.

40
00:03:35,317 --> 00:03:37,674
Position the camera at the entrance.

41
00:03:37,674 --> 00:03:42,390
Create markers for the entrance, the studio
viewpoint and the performance center.

42
00:03:47,317 --> 00:03:50,352
Add the tour controller to an empty GameObject.

43
00:03:50,352 --> 00:03:54,526
Assign the camera, environment, performer and
location markers in the Inspector.

44
00:03:54,526 --> 00:03:59,078
Set separate entry and exit distances to avoid
flickering at the boundary.

45
00:04:09,317 --> 00:04:11,719
Movement follows the camera heading.

46
00:04:11,719 --> 00:04:16,042
The proximity check controls the performer’s
visibility and playback.

47
00:04:16,042 --> 00:04:18,444
Teleportation applies the saved viewpoint.

48
00:04:23,317 --> 00:04:24,070
Press Play.

49
00:04:24,070 --> 00:04:29,347
We start at the entrance, move through the
environment, and teleport to the studio.

50
00:04:29,347 --> 00:04:32,739
The performer appears when we enter the viewing
range.

51
00:04:36,317 --> 00:04:39,614
Move around the performer while the sequence
keeps playing.

52
00:04:39,614 --> 00:04:42,544
View it from the front, side and rear.

53
00:04:42,544 --> 00:04:46,574
Notice how the person, instrument and background
change with the viewpoint.
